There are several ways that thieves may be able to break into your locked car. First, they may use a variety of tools to pick your lock, which may include simple lock picking devices or complex lock picking skills. Another possibility is they may use force to break a window or dashboard panel, either with their hands or specialized tools like a crowbar or hammer. Another possibility is they may use technology to circumvent your lock: they may use a device to send a signal to your vehicle's electrical system to unlock it or use software to copy your key fob's radio signals to open your lock without your actual key being present. To reduce the chance of thievery, you can: 

- Keep your vehicle locked whenever it is not in use; 
- Cover your vehicle's windows when it is parked; 
- Lock any valuable items inside your trunk; 
- Replace any vulnerable exterior vehicle parts (door locks, brake and door lights) with higher security locks;
- Do not keep your keys in plain view inside your vehicle or hide them easily accessible spots.

